<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11864"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, March 19, 2005 - 10:37 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I don&#39;t think these guy&#39;s did it for a living. The pilot was apparently not even rated &#40;been trained&#41; on the plane he was flying, and the plane was designed for eight passengers. They had 4000 lbs of pot. Each passenger is usually estimated at 200lb &#43; 50 lbs personal effects. They had the equivalent of 500 lbs per seat/ twice the weight limit.  <BR> <BR>The number two engine actually burst into flame because of an exhaust valve problem, meaning the plane was not being proffessionaly maintained. Professional drug smugglers make sure they have reliable planes and experienced pilots. That was a loss of merchandise worth around $150000 to $200000. Plus if it weren&#39;t such an amateur looking attempt, the cops would have tried to trace the plane to a key figure.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11864-->
<!--Post: 11885--><!--Time: 1111353026-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11885"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, March 20, 2005 - 1:10 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->B, <BR> <BR>Normally I would agree but back in the old days of pot smuggling, according to Long, they were very unprofessional. Long actually flew his DC3 and he was not rated. In the beginning they did not do flight checks as they were trying to hide their planes until they left the airstrip. <BR> <BR>Long crashed his plane by overloading and lack of maintenance. Even George Jung flew one mission into Mexico almost killing himself in the process.  <BR> <BR>My thought is that if this was 1974 there were very few guys that did not know ea other in this smuggler&#39;s community. Most were getting pot from Columbia&#39;s Santa Marta region or Mexican badlands.  .  .   <BR> <BR>Back in 1974 cops were unsophisticated and rarely could trace planes as these guys would buy from consolidators and then strip the tail #s.  <BR> <BR>In addition these rigs were flown out of very rural out of the way tiny airports where the owners were often bought off or closed mouth.  .  .   <BR> <BR>LE did not catch on to Long until one of his first huge loads of 4,080lbs was discovered on a desolate Virginia runway in 1976.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
