<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11977"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, March 21, 2005 - 10:11 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->B, <BR> <BR>Another of Long&#39;s pilots was &#34;Easy&#34; Mathews based in Virginia. Mathews/Long/Carlyle operations were based in Jupiter, Fla. They moved 20,000lbs their first load and then nothing less than 40,000lbs per flight from then on out.  .  . the problem is that this was in 1977. But IMO Long or Jung or any of their associates may have know the men in the crash.  .  .   <BR> <BR>One major reason for this thinking is that once smugglers began using planes around &#39;73, trained pilots or guys crazy enough to try &#40;without much training&#41; were a very VALUABLE commodity. <BR> <BR>According to Jung and Long pilots were often like free agents in sports going from one smuggler to a higher bidding smuggler.  .  .   <BR> <BR>Now if our 3 crash victims were very inexperienced in flight skills SOMEONE amongst the 3 or one of their associates had a major pot connection. So in essence they were playing in the big leagues.  .  .   <BR> <BR>The ultimate answer&#40;s&#41; may lie in finding the crash vic&#39;s true identities and their &#34;front money&#34; man. The man with the money or the man that set up the deal with Columbian dealers IMO would have the real information.  .  .  <BR> <BR>Btw if the flight plan, if they were stupid enough to have filed one, stated a starting point of Puerto Rico ?  Or was that the NTSB&#39;s investigation ?  <BR> <BR>Most likely if the plane was thought to have taken off from Puerto Rico that would have been a refueling stop. The Bahamas or Columbia would have been where they scored.  .  .  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11977-->
<!--Post: 11979--><!--Time: 1111477078-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11979"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, March 21, 2005 - 11:37 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->No flight plan. They didn&#39;t even check the weather as far as the NTSB or FAA could determine &#40; at least not through a flight service station, they might have watched Willard Scott&#41;. <BR> <BR>They were flying under instrument conditions &#40;bad visibility, haze&#41;when the #2 engine developed problems. Instead of feathering the engine they continued to run it till it caught fire. As they turned on final approach the airspeed was too slow &#40; they had to approach low because of low ceiling&#41; and the turn was enough to stall the wings and they nosed in and burned on impact. <BR> <BR>They were pretty much screwed after the engine caught fire ititially, unless they would have lucked out and had a straight approach to the runway.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11979-->
<!--Post: 12062--><!--Time: 1111543064-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12062"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 22, 2005 - 5:57 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->B, <BR> <BR>&#34;As the crow flys&#34; was the navigational method of those days. Most pilots that were inexperienced flew by compass and directions they asked at their last stop. Frightening.  .  .   <BR> <BR>Another huge pot kingpin is still alive today: Paul Hindelang. He actually paid the Govt $50mil penalty not long ago. He spent time in jail and is said to be one of the wealthiest kingpins to leave the trade and keep his money. <BR> <BR>He was active in the mid 1970s.  .  . he is now living in LA. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
