<!--Post: 12064--><!--Time: 1111543722-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12064"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 22, 2005 - 6:08 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->B, <BR> <BR>Here is another possible avenue. According to Long&#39;s book the biggest source of used planes being purchased by smugglers of the era was the magazine &#34;Trade-a-Plane&#34;. From what I can see it is a publication based in Crossville, Tenn est. in 1937. The Logo is a Planet with &#34;Trade-A-Plane&#34; banner in the middle. <BR> <BR>According to the book this is where many smugglers bought their first planes.  .  .  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12064-->
