<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12310"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, March 27, 2005 - 3:06 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->B, <BR> <BR>I concur 100%. If you are going to dig into CIA drug dealing the best article was written a few years ago by a San Jose Bee journalist blowing the whistle on the CIA&#39;s aleged crack dealing in South Central LA.  .  .   <BR> <BR>As for Allen Long the pot smuggler, I have chronicled, the latest news is that he and author Robt Sabbag are pitching a script for a movie.  <BR> <BR>A studio is already in place. Rumor has it that Brad Pitt and Matt McConehey are clamouring for the part of Long but the studio wants a younger actor.  <BR> <BR>Either way this movie will do for Long what &#34;Blow&#34; did for George Jung. It will shake out a lot of articles and information hopefully.  .  .   <BR> <BR>The problem with researching info on plane crashes is that many of these crashes were never recorded as the planes were unregistered, pilots unlicensed, and bodies often &#34;vanished&#34;. <BR> <BR>Long &amp; Jung both feared, more than anything else, the last legs of trips. When coming in off the Atlantic their planes often would be no more than 60 ft off the water with no lights. Many planes in less than ideal conditions slammed into large tankers, squashed like bugs, never to be seen again.  .  .     <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
