<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12378"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 29, 2005 - 2:35 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Noriega, as history documented, was the weigh station for Escobar back in the 80&#39;s. The CIA and US Govt both knew and approved Noriega&#39;s activities as a way to keep an eye on things.  .  .   <BR> <BR>In reality moving blow through govt airplanes is old hat. Diplomats to the US from S. America have been using their diplomatic immunity, which includes no customs or security checks, to bring multiple kilos in through their personal luggage and steamer trunks.  .  .   <BR> <BR>Here in Northern California back in the late 1970s and on through coke&#39;s hey day of the 1980&#39;s, stories of diplomats and airplane/cargo shipments was common knowledge to both the public and &#34;Johnny Law.&#34;  The difference back then was that both coke and pot were viewed as harmless compared to alcohol. Due to this ideology, many prominent LE ie attorney/police/inspectors were also involved in consumption and smuggling of the aforementioned.  .  .  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
